  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 244 PM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nAn upper ridge of high pressure extends from the southeast part of \nthe country and Texas northward to Nebraska and portions of the \nMidwest. An upper low is centered over southern Canada and extends \nsouthward over the northern Plains and portions of the Upper \nMidwest. Winds across south central and central Nebraska and north \ncentral Kansas are fairly light at around 5 to 10 MPH and are \ntransitioning to the south to southeast. High temperatures today \nwill range from the mid 80s to upper 90s across the forecast area. A \nshortwave trough is expected to move over/near the area late this \nevening into tonight. This will result in showers and storms \ndeveloping and moving across mainly areas along and to the west and \nnorth of the Tri-Cities area beginning around 1 AM and continuing \nuntil around or slightly past sunrise. CAPE, wind shear, and lapse \nrates will be sufficient for some of these storms to become strong \nto marginally severe. Hail up to around quarter size and wind gusts \nup to around 60 MPH will be possible with the strongest storms. A \nsurface low will be near/across the area tonight with low \ntemperatures in the mid 60s to mid 70s. \n\nA surface low will remain across the area on Tuesday with south \ncentral and central Nebraska and north central Kansas on the \nnorthwest periphery of the upper ridge. Winds will increase out of \nmostly the south Tuesday afternoon with highs warming up into the \nupper 80s to near 100 degrees. A weak cold front will begin moving \ninto the area Tuesday evening into Tuesday night. Showers and storms \nare expected to develop late Tuesday afternoon/early Tuesday evening \nalong this front and associated with an upper disturbance moving \nover/near the area. These storms will have the potential to become \nstrong to severe given high CAPE and fairly high wind shear and \nlapse rates. Large hail and damaging winds will be the main threats. \nThe severe threat will last until around midnight with storms \nexpected to go away 2 to 3 hours afterwards. Low temperatures \nTuesday night will generally range from the low/mid 60s to the \nmid/upper 70s. \n\nWinds will increase out of the south Wednesday afternoon as a \nsurface trough strengthens across western Nebraska and western \nKansas. High temperatures on Wednesday will be similar or slightly \nhigher than the previous day. Isolated showers and storms may move \nacross northern portions of the area (mainly north of the Tri-\nCities) Wednesday evening into Wednesday night as a shortwave moves \noverhead. The region will continue to be under the northwestern \nperiphery of the upper ridge through Friday before the ridge become \nmore centered over the southern/central Plains over the weekend. On \nand off again slight rain and storm chances (15% to 25%) will be \npresent across various portions of the area through Sunday night. \nHigh temperatures Thursday through Sunday will generally range from \nthe upper 80s to low triple digits.",
